Create VLANs: Why do we need VLANs
----------------------------------
suppose Finance and HR are using the same switch

    Without VLANs:
    -------------
    Finance PC --------\
                     \
                      Switch
                     /
    HR PC -------------/

    - everyone is one broadcast domain
    - if finance sends a broadcast, HR also receives it,
    - that's inefficient and can be security concern

Now create two VLANs:
---------------------
enable
(cisco have different privilege levels)
(user mode > lets you view limited information)

config terminal
(this command enters Global Configuration Mode)

vlan 10
(this create a new Layer2 broadcast domain with VLAN ID 10)
(this of it as creating a new department)

name FINANCE
(the switch only needs the VLAN ID to function,
the name is for humans, making it easier to indentify VLAN later)
exit

vlan 20
name HR
exit

Verify configs:
---------------
sh vlan brief (should show default vlan and created vlans)



************* Understand what did we actually create *****************
                One Physical Switch
          +----------------------------+
          |                            |
          |   VLAN 10    |   VLAN 20   |
          |   Finance    |     HR      |
          |              |             |
          +----------------------------+
          * one physical switch is now behaving like two independent switches
          * instead of buying 3 physical switches, we are using 1 physical switches as diff logical switches
          * they behave like a as if they are 3 different switches

          Q) Does creating a VLAN automatically assign ports to it: NO
             - creating a VLAN only creates the VLAN database entry
             - no ports belong to it until we explicitly assign them
